{"id": "208235", "revid": "1092128", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208235", "title": "\u017belazowa Wola", "text": "\u017belazowa Wola is a village in Mazowieckie. It is a day-trip destination mostly known for being the birthplace of Fr\u00e9d\u00e9ric Chopin.\nUnderstand.\n\u017belazowa Wola is a manor village in the fields of Mazowieckie, relatively close to Kampinos forest. This generally insignificant place is known mostly as the birthplace of Fryderyk Chopin, who was born here in 1810. His family moved soon after his birth to Warsaw, but they occasionally returned to the estate, owned by the Skarbek family, for holidays. The house is the only building on the estate to have survived to the present day.\nThe manor often changed owners, and was not well maintained. In 1894 the location was recognized, and a monument to Fr\u00e9d\u00e9ric Chopin was erected in its park. During World War I, the right outbuilding was burnt after a lightning strike. In 1928, the manor was bought by various Chopin societies and after World War II, it was turned into a museum, which also hosts musical events.\nGet in.\nBy car.\n\u017belazowa Wola is on the road which is easily reached by detour north from roads and (Wiskitki exit). There is a paid parking by the manor and park.\nBy public transport.\nYou will have to reach Sochaczew by train first either by PKP Intercity or Koleje Mazowieckie, then you can use free bus line number 6 from train station to \u017belazowa Wola. Bus only operates from May to September, you can check timetable here.\nGet around.\nManor with park is the only sight in the area, and is easily manageable on foot. The entire village is not much larger than the park.\nBuy.\nThere is a gift shop in the Chopin Museum visitors' centre.\nSleep.\nThere are no hotels in \u017belazowa Wola, however you may have more luck in nearby Sochaczew or Warsaw. Some expensive apartments are available in nearby villages."}
{"id": "208280", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208280", "title": "Pyu city-states", "text": "The Pyu city-states were a group of independent city-states established in what is now Myanmar. The Pyu civilisation stretched over a millennium from 2nd century BCE to the mid-11th century and marked the beginning of Burmese recorded history.\nUnderstand.\nThe Pyu had migrated to the Irrawaddy Delta by the 2nd century BCE. This region was part of the overland trade route between India and China and was consequently influenced by both of its neighbouring civilisations. The Pyu adopted Theravada Buddhism as their main religion. Chinese records at the time note that Pyu culture was humane and peaceful.\nThe Pyu people spoke a Sino-Tibetan language, which was distantly related to Chinese, Tibetan and Burmese. However, the Burmans were a distinct ethnic group who had established themselves in the Irrawaddy Delta by the mid 9th century and over time replaced the Pyu culture that had been dominant in the region.\nAncient cities.\nThree of the walled and fortified Pyu cities have been extensively excavated and since 2014 have been recognised as UNESCO World Heritages Sites. "}
{"id": "208300", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208300", "title": "Parc del Castell de Montesquiu", "text": "Parc del Castell de Montesquiu is in Barcelona (province), Catalonia, Spain. Is a natural park with some Castles and buildings in northern Barcelona province\nUnderstand.\nIt is located in the eastern Pre-Pyrenean region, at the northern end of the Osona comarca, and covers parts of the municipalities of Montesquiu, Sant Quirze de Besora, Santa Maria de Besora, and Sora. The park has an area of 546.45 hectares, and its territory is divided into two parts by the course of the Ter River. It features elevations that range between 580 and 850 meters.\nHistory.\nLocated between the mountains of Ripoll\u00e8s and Cerdanya and the plains of Osona, the area of the Parc has been an ideal location for human settlement since ancient times. The most notable architectural remains date back to the early Middle Ages. Montesquiu Castle itself, the chapel of Sant Mo\u00ed, the remains of the Llaers road, the Codines bridge, and evidence of the primitive strada are the most significant medieval constructions in the park.\nWhile certain farmhouses like Codines, Planeses, Casanova, Sant Mo\u00ed, Solana, and the castle\u2019s farmhouse have medieval origins, all of them underwent significant transformations or were rebuilt during the 18th century.\nMontesquiu Castle is the most notable monument in the park.\nLandscape.\nThe landscape of Parc del Castell de Montesquiu is characterized by a terrain traversed by several east-west mountain ranges, notably the Bufadors Range and the Serrat de la Rovira. The area features forests of oaks and Scots pines, as well as old farmhouses, some of which have been adapted for educational activities or to maintain agricultural practices. However, the most distinctive element of the park is the Montesquiu Castle, which gives the park its name.\nThe landscape of the park has been deeply shaped by human activity. Four main units can be identified: forests, meadows, riparian vegetation, and cultivated fields. The predominant forest is composed of pubescent oak (Quercus pubescens). In many areas, Scots pine (Pinus sylvestris) dominates. Generally, the oak grows on sunny slopes and plains, while the Scots pine thrives in shaded areas. There are also patches of typical Central European vegetation, including beeches and linden trees. The dense forest provides shelter for a diverse range of wildlife.\nRiparian vegetation forms narrow bands along the riverbanks and includes a wide variety of species such as elm, poplar, willow, alder, and plane trees. This environment supports a rich diversity of fauna.\nThe cultivated fields, located around the main farmhouses, are primarily used for forage and cereal crops. Along the banks of the Ter River, terraces are dedicated to vegetable farming.\nClimate.\nThe climate is typically Mediterranean with continental influences, characterized by mild to warm summers and cool winters. Average temperatures range between 5\u00b0C in winter and 25\u00b0C in summer. Rainfall is moderate and fairly distributed throughout the year, with spring and autumn being the wettest seasons. Snowfall can occur in winter, especially at higher elevations. The varied terrain and forest cover create localized microclimates, with shaded valleys remaining cooler and more humid than sunny slopes. These conditions support diverse vegetation, including oak, Scots pine, and riparian forests, and sustain a rich variety of wildlife.\nFees and permits.\nFree to visit the park. Some activities has a fee, like the guided visit to the castle.\nDo.\nHiking.\nthere are several marked routes:"}
{"id": "208301", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208301", "title": "Coscomatepec", "text": "Coscomatepec is a small, historically Totonac town in the mountains of Central Veracruz. Visitors come for the storied colonial downtown area of town, or perhaps to see (or maybe to climb) Pico de Orizaba, an active volcano and the highest mountain in Mexico. The town is one of Mexico's designated Pueblos M\u00e1gicos.\nUnderstand.\nThe town is officially, but never actually called \"Coscomatepec del Bravo\", the town is usually referred to more colloquially as \"Cosco\". It was originally settled in 1325 by the Totonac people, an indigenous culture believed to have descended from the people of Teotihuacan. The town paid heavy tributes to the Aztec empire until the conquest. The Spanish arrived in town in 1597 to establish their dominion. In 1620, they moved 60 enslaved families from Mexico City to the town. Coscomatepec was a significant site in the Mexican War of Independence when General Nicolas de Bravo, leading 600 freedom fighters, stormed the Spanish stronghold in the wee hours of October 4, 1813.\nGet in.\nThe best way to get to Coscomatepec is to take an ADO or AU bus to C\u00f3rdoba and then take a regional bus or a taxi to Coscomatepec. ADO and AU operate several buses per day to Cordoba, which takes about 2 hours and costs about M$100 one way. From Cordoba, a taxi will take about 30 minutes and cost about M$200. Astro buses to Coscomatepec are cheaper but only depart a couple times per day.\nBy car.\nCoscomatepec is 28 km northwest of C\u00f3rdoba via federal highway MEX-143. The drive will take about 50 minutes.\nGet around.\nThe town is fairly compact and easily navigated by walking or using a bicycle. The street layout is a bit chaotic though and can be confusing because many streets are both numbered and named. For example, Calle 1 Ignacio Zaragoza or Avenida 8 Hermanos Galeana. \nBuy.\nTravelers who appreciate Artesanal hand-crafts will find a number of shops selling locally made leather goods, particularly saddles, belts, wallets and leather bags.\nMonday is market day in Coscomatepec, and although there is no permanent market, a \"tianguis\" is set up starting at about 7am on Calle 1 Ignacio Zaragoza with numerous vendors selling fresh produce, meats (including rabbit, sheep, and goat), and a wide range of prepared regional dishes.\nThe town is known for its artesanal cigar makers. The city's tourism department can arrange a tour to visit the workshops of various craftsmen, most of whom work out of their own homes. Tobacco is brought into town, treated with vanilla and liquors, and dried before being rolled into cigars (\"puros\") by the craftsmen.\nEat.\nCoscomatepec's gastronomy derives mostly from common dishes of Central Mexico with occasional local variations, such as \"barbacoa blanco\", which is pit-cooked chicken spiced with a guajillo chile based marinade (barbacoas elsewhere in Mexico are made using pork).\nA regional specialty associated with Coscomatepec is \"tlatonile\". This local dish is a type of mole made with chile comape\u00f1o, Ccile ancho, sesame seeds and epazote. The town is also famous for its baked goods and if you get a chance, it would be worth stopping in at one of their several bakeries."}
{"id": "208302", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208302", "title": "Ambler (Alaska)", "text": "Ambler, also known by its Inupiaq name Ivisaappaat, is a small but vibrant community in Arctic Alaska. It is rich in culture and history on the north bank of the Kobuk River, near the confluence of the Ambler and the Kobuk Rivers. It lies 45 mi north of the Arctic Circle. \nUnderstand.\nAmbler was permanently settled in 1958 when people from Shungnak and Kobuk moved downstream because of the variety of fish, wild game and spruce trees in the area. Ambler was home to 274 people in 2020, the large majority of whom are I\u00f1upiat (Alaskan Natives). \nClimate.\nAmbler has a subarctic climate characterized by extremely cold winters and short, mild summers. Winter temperatures can drop below -40 \u00b0F, while summer highs can reach up to 80 \u00b0F. The best time to visit is during late spring to early fall (May to September) when the weather is more temperate, and the outdoor activities are abundant.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nAmbler is remote and does not have road access to other parts of Alaska. The primary mode of transportation to and from Ambler is by air. \nBy boat.\nDuring the summer months (early June to mid-October), small boats navigate the Kobuk River, making it another option for reaching Ambler and exploring the surrounding areas.\nGet around.\nAmbler\u2019s major means of transportation are plane, small boat, and snow machine. Dog sleds are useful too. There are no roads linking the city to other parts of the state. \nBoats are used for inter-village travel and subsistence activities, while ATVs and snow machines are commonly used in winter.\nDo.\nFish for chum salmon and hunt caribou in season.\nBuy.\nArtisan shops offer local handicrafts, including birch baskets, fur pelts, and carvings made from jade, quartz, bone, and ivory.\nEat and drink.\nExperience the tastes of Alaska with local fish, caribou, and seasonal berries. While dining options are limited, local stores may offer fresh fish and traditional foods. "}
{"id": "208324", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208324", "title": "Central Asian cuisine", "text": "The cuisine of Central Asia reflects its history and cultural influences with its Turko-Mongol nomadic heritage, Silk Road connections, Persian and Russian rule and Islamic dietary laws all shaping the food eaten in the region. You will find common dishes throughout Central Asia as well as dishes unique to one or two countries. \nCountries and regions.\nGenerally the further south you go, the more flavorful the food becomes with greater use of spices in Afghanistan and Tajikistan.\nNoodle-based dishes are more popular in Kyrgyzstan, reflecting its closer ties to China.\nIngredients.\nCentral Asian cuisine relies heavily on meat and dairy products. The high level of consumption of horse meat in particular distinguishes the region from much of the rest of the world.\nSome types of food \u2014 kebabs, flatbreads and a soft cheese like Greek \"feta\" or Indian \"panir\" \u2014 are common everywhere between Greece and northern China, and in the Indian subcontinent and all the Arab countries, though the details vary considerably from place to place. Central Asia has local variants of all those.\nThe region has a profusion of melons and a very wide range of types. Some towns, such as Hami in Xinjiang, are widely known for their melons."}
{"id": "208329", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208329", "title": "Marlborough (Queensland)", "text": "\"Not to be confused with Maryborough (Queensland).\"\nMarlborough is a small town of 150 (2021) in Central Queensland along the Bruce Highway between Rockhampton and Mackay. It doesn't have much going for it, but it is the only town for a decent bit as the section of highway between Rocky and Mackay contain a fair bit of nothingness and makes for a good place to break the drive.\nGet in.\nMarlborough is 106 km north of Rockhampton on the A1 Bruce Hwy after which you'll approach an intersection (though do pay attention to signage \u2013 it's very easy to miss when driving at 100 km/h!) between Bruce Hwy, Marlborough Rd and Glenprarie Bypass Road. Turn right onto Glenprarie Bypass Rd and then onto Perkins Rd and Fossey Dr into Marlborough. If you're headed southbound, the drive is 230 km from Mackay and 173 km from Koumala. Instead turn left at Glenprarie Bypass Road and follow the same directions as if headed northbound (also pay attention to signage southbound, even more so than northbound, as it's very easy to miss at 110 km/h).\nA train station exists but sees no scheduled rail services. Nonetheless it is still served by Greyhound, though there is not much of a reason to stop here if on Greyhound.\nCope.\nA Caltex is available on Perkins Road, but only has diesel, 91 and 98 octane (no E10/94 or 95). However, only use this in extreme circumstances \u2013 your wallet will thank you for filling up in Rockhampton or Mackay."}
{"id": "208345", "revid": "76122", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208345", "title": "Hatoma", "text": "Hatoma (\u9ce9\u9593\u5cf6 \"Hatoma-jima\") is a tiny flyspeck of an island north of Iriomote in the Yaeyama Islands of southern Okinawa, Japan.\nUnderstand.\nBlink and you'll miss it: Hatoma has an area of under 1 square kilometer and a population of under 50. Once a tuna fishing port, the only industry these days is catering to tourists who \"really\" want to get away from it all.\nHatoma had a brief moment on the national stage in 2005 when TV drama \"Lapis Lazuli Island\" (\u7460\u7483\u306e\u5cf6 \"Ruri no Shima\") aired, based on the true story of the island's school which was in danger of closing down, but managed to reinvent itself as a boarding school for children from the mainland who were struggling in normal schools. The school, and the \"Kaihin Ry\u016bgaku\" (Seaside Exchange Student) program, continue to operate to this day.\nGet in.\nThe only way in is by ferry. Anei Kanko and Yaeyama Kanko operate two ferries each every day from Ishigaki (45 min), one of which stops at Uehara on Iriomote (15 min). Prices are identical on both and the journey from Ishigaki costs \u00a55,170 return (Dec 2024). All services may be cancelled if there's a strong north wind, so check the forecast and allow a little flexibility in your plans.\nGet around.\nHatoma is small enough to walk around in one hour. Everything in Hatoma is within easy striking distance of the, but rental bicycles are also available.\nSee and do.\nHatoma's sole draws are the beaches and snorkeling.\nEat and drink.\nEating options open to the public on Hatoma are very limited. Most overnight guests eat at their lodgings.\nSleep.\nAccommodation options on Hatoma are limited to simple pension-style guesthouses. Most visitors opt to day-trip from Ishigaki."}
{"id": "208397", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208397", "title": "Gold Coast Hinterland", "text": "The Gold Coast Hinterland is a rural area in the west of Gold Coast. It consists of mountain ranges covered in rainforests, most of which are national parks. For Wikivoyage purposes, Springbrook, Numinbah Valley and Tamborine Mountain are covered separately.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nDriving to any part of the hinterland is easy by car. Simply take the 99, 95, 90, 92 or 97 routes off the M1.\nBy public transport.\nThe public transport network west of the M1 is very sparse. The hourly 748 operates from 6:20AM to 5:20PM."}
{"id": "208398", "revid": "155858", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208398", "title": "Mangshi", "text": "Mangshi (Dai language: \u195b\u196b\u1952\u1970 \u1951\u1969\u1962\u1974, meaning \"City of Dawn\") is a city nestled along the China-Myanmar border, that is a microcosm of Yunnan's rich ethnic minority cultures and a vital gateway to Southeast Asia. This city is the perfect place to immerse yourself in the traditional cultures of the Dai and Jingpo people. Its warm and welcoming residents, vibrant festivals, and unique architectural styles promise to leave you enchanted.\nUnderstand.\nOrientation.\nMangshi is located in the southwestern part of Yunnan Province, China, and serves as the capital of the Dehong Dai and Jingpo Autonomous Prefecture. The city is situated in a typical subtropical monsoon climate zone, surrounded by mountains and natural beauty. It is an important gateway to Southeast Asia, bordering Myanmar, making it an ideal destination for travelers.\nName.\nThe name \"Mangshi\" comes from the Dai language and means \"City of Dawn.\" This name reflects the city\u2019s significance in Dai culture and symbolizes its vitality and hope for the future.\nHistory.\nMangshi has a long and rich history that dates back to the Ming and Qing dynasties. It has long been an important hub for transportation and trade between Yunnan and Myanmar. Due to its unique geographical location, Mangshi has historically been a center where multiple ethnic cultures intersect. It has been home to the Dai and Jingpo people, whose cultures and lifestyles have greatly influenced the social development of Mangshi. The city has served as an important political and military base in the Dehong region, holding strategic significance throughout various dynasties.\nClimate.\nMangshi has a typical subtropical monsoon climate, with warm and humid weather year-round and distinct seasons. During spring and autumn, the temperatures are pleasant, usually ranging from 20\u00b0C to 25\u00b0C during the day, with cooler nights, making it ideal for outdoor activities and travel. Summer is warm and humid, with temperatures reaching around 30\u00b0C, and rainfall is heavy, especially from June to September, with frequent showers or thunderstorms. Due to Mangshi's higher elevation, the heat of summer is somewhat alleviated compared to lowland cities. Winter is warm and dry, with temperatures generally ranging from 10\u00b0C to 20\u00b0C during the day and cooler nights. Occasional cold fronts may bring a slight chill, but the overall temperature remains mild. With abundant rainfall throughout the year, the city has high humidity, which supports lush plant growth, and the surrounding natural landscape is beautifully green. Overall, Mangshi's climate is very pleasant, resembling spring year-round, making it an ideal destination for travel at any time of the year.\nRead.\nKey readings about Mangshi include works such as Yunnan Ethnic Culture and History and Culture of Dehong. These books offer detailed insights into the history, cultural heritage, and ethnic diversity of Mangshi. They are particularly valuable for visitors interested in learning about the region\u2019s rich minority cultures.\nWatch.\nAlthough Mangshi is not often the focus of major travel documentaries, it is featured in several documentaries about Yunnan and minority cultures. For instance, the documentaries The Ethnic Charm of Yunnan and Stories from the Border showcase the natural beauty and diverse cultures of Mangshi and its surroundings.\nVisitor information.\nMangshi tourist information[dead link]\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nFor flights to Mangshi, travelers can book tickets through the airport's official website or various local travel agents in Mangshi and nearby areas. Tickets for domestic flights are typically around \u00a5600 to 1500 depending on the destination and time of booking (Dec 2024).\nBy train.\nThere is as yet no train station in Mangshi as of February 2025. The line from Baoshan to Ruili via Mangshi is expected to open in the next few years.\nBy bus.\nMangshi has a central bus station that serves destinations within Yunnan Province and neighboring regions. The station is in the city center and offers services to places such as Ruili and Kunming, as well as several smaller towns and local destinations. Long-distance buses from Mangshi are generally reliable, but it is recommended to check the buses for cleanliness and comfort before buying tickets, especially for overnight services.\nFor international travel, buses to Myanmar and Laos are available, although services may not always be running. The journey to Myanmar's border city of Ruili is straightforward, with buses regularly departing from Mangshi. The trip to Laos (via Mohan) requires a longer journey, but it is possible to travel from Mangshi to Vientiane or other major cities in Laos.\nBuses to these international destinations are often cheaper than flights, but travel times can be long. Always check for the availability of the services before planning your trip, as schedules may vary. There are also occasional stops at local restaurants during the journey, offering a chance to rest and enjoy some local food.\nThe road network in and around Mangshi is well-developed, with regular bus services connecting the city to major destinations in Yunnan and beyond:\nThere are also local shuttle buses connecting Mangshi with nearby towns and cities in Dehong Prefecture. The fare for these short-distance buses typically ranges from \u00a520 to 50, depending on the destination (Dec 2024).\nBy car.\nDriving is one of the best ways to explore Mangshi and its surrounding areas, especially for travelers who enjoy flexible travel schedules. Here are a few recommended routes:\nGet around.\nMangshi, the capital of Dehong Dai and Jingpo Autonomous Prefecture, is a small yet well-connected city, making it easy for visitors to explore its charm through various means of transportation. Here are some common ways to get around the city and its surroundings:\nBy bus.\nMangshi has a central bus station located in the city center, with regular departures to various destinations within Dehong Prefecture and other parts of Yunnan Province. The bus services are generally reliable, affordable, and comfortable, though some longer journeys may have basic facilities. Most buses depart from the main bus terminal near the city center, and there are several buses throughout the day.\nFor travel within Yunnan, buses connect Mangshi to neighboring towns such as Ruili (about 1.5 hours), Dali (approximately 6-7 hours), and Kunming (around 8-9 hours). There are also buses that go to nearby border cities like Jiegao (on the Myanmar border) and international destinations.\nInternational bus services, while less frequent, are also available. Buses from Mangshi connect to Ruili (close to the Myanmar border), and there are occasional routes that run between Mangshi and Vientiane in Laos. Travelers should be prepared for long travel times and be aware that these international bus services may not always operate on a regular schedule.\nLocal buses and minibuses are the primary mode of transportation for getting around within the city and its immediate surroundings. These services are frequent, inexpensive, and cover key areas of the city, making it easy to navigate Mangshi.\nLocal buses.\nMangshi has several bus routes that provide convenient transportation within the city. The bus routes, including Line 1, Line 2, Line 3, and Line 5, cover key residential areas, commercial centers, and tourist spots. Fares typically range from \u00a51 to 3, with buses departing every 10 minutes to an hour. In addition to city buses, there are airport shuttle buses that connect the city to Dehong Mangshi Airport, making it easy for travelers and residents to reach the airport. The departure times and fares for different bus routes may vary, so it is recommended to check the latest timetables and fare information before traveling.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis in Mangshi start at \u00a58 (Dec 2024). Make sure the driver uses the meter to avoid being scammed, as sometimes taxi drivers may ask for a flat fee, which is usually higher than the metered fare for short trips within the city. As is typical, it's advisable to take care when traveling at night or over long distances. Larger taxis typically cost more than smaller ones. Additionally, taxis in Mangshi are zoned and may not be able to access all areas. Don't be surprised if a driver tells you they cannot take you to your destination, as it might be outside their designated area. Only certain taxis are allowed to enter central areas at specific times, to ensure all drivers have a chance at more lucrative business. It can also be difficult to get a taxi around 18:30 when the shift change occurs and drivers need to return their cars. A fuel tax of \u00a53 has been added to fares, and you\u2019ll see a small sign on the dashboard explaining this. For each ride, add \u00a53 to the meter reading to calculate the total fare.\nBy bicycle.\nBicycles can be rented in some hostels for about \u00a520 a day (Dec 2024). There are bike lanes along many major roads in Mangshi, and traffic is generally not very fast, making cycling a pleasant option for getting around. In addition to traditional bike rentals, Mangshi also has a growing number of shared bicycles, available for rent via mobile apps. The shared bike system is convenient and allows you to pick up and drop off bikes at various locations around the city, including popular areas like the Dianchi Lake region. The cost for using shared bikes typically starts at around \u00a51 per half hour, and you can easily rent and return bikes using the app. However, registration is required, and it may not be as accessible to short-term foreign tourists unless they have a Chinese phone number or local ID for verification. Shared bikes provide an affordable and flexible way to explore the city at a leisurely pace.\nEat.\nTry rice noodle dishes, such as \"Mixian,\" a popular local rice noodle soup served with a variety of meats and vegetables. The noodles are often served with spicy and sour broth, reflecting the region's blend of Chinese and Southeast Asian influences.\nExplore the local street food scene in Mangshi, where you can find skewers of grilled meat, fresh spring rolls, and other delicious snacks. The street food in Mangshi is known for its rich flavors and variety, and the night markets are particularly lively with numerous food stalls offering affordable and tasty meals.\nDon\u2019t miss out on traditional desserts such as \"sticky rice with mango\" or other tropical fruit-based sweets. Local cafes and dessert shops offer unique offerings that highlight the region's tropical ingredients.\nDrink.\nTry traditional Dai tea, known for its unique flavors and health benefits. You can find it at local tea houses or stalls. It's often served with a side of local snacks, providing a delightful cultural experience.\nFruit juices and smoothies: Given the tropical climate, fresh fruit juices made from local fruits like mango, pineapple, and passion fruit are popular. You can find many vendors offering these refreshing drinks, perfect for cooling off after a day of exploration.\nBeer and spirits: Sample local brews and spirits, such as the rice wine known as \"jiu,\" which is often homemade and varies by region. This drink is commonly found in local restaurants and is a great accompaniment to traditional meals."}
{"id": "208401", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208401", "title": "Montes Azules Biosphere Reserve", "text": "Montes Azules Biosphere Reserve (\"Reserva de la Bi\u00f3sfera Montes Azules\") is a protected natural area of more than 330,000 hectares of tropical jungle in the state of Chiapas in southern Mexico. It is part of the UNESCO World Network of Biosphere Reserves. \nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe jungles are the traditional home of several Mayan indigenous groups, particularly the Lacondon Maya. Many archaeological sites are in the jungles including major tourist draws at Palenque, Yaxchilan and Bonampak. The biosphere reserve was established in 1978.\nLandscape.\nThe vast jungle is known as the \"Lacandon jungle\", which is home to more than 100 species of mammal, including some big cats like the jaguar and puma, more than 390 species of birds, and dozens of snakes and other reptiles (including the Mexican crocodile, which lurks in the swamps and lakes). The jungle is wild and undeveloped, but is under pressure from growing populations in the region who are constantly encroaching on the jungle to clear cut land for farms or ranches. \nFlora and fauna.\nThe \"Lacandon jungle\" is home to more than 100 species of mammal, including some big cats like the jaguar and puma, more than 390 species of birds, and dozens of snakes and other reptiles (including the Mexican crocodile, which lurks in the swamps and lakes). More than 1,500 plant species have been identified in the reserve.\nStay safe.\nThis is dense tropical jungle. Take steps to prevent malaria and other insect-borne tropical diseases. Use mosquito repellent containing DEET. Wear long pants and sturdy hiking boots. Watch for venomous snakes and other dangerous animals."}
{"id": "208415", "revid": "2441240", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208415", "title": "Jinhua", "text": "Jinhua City (\u91d1\u534e, pronounced [t\u0255\u00edn.xw\u00e1] in Mandarin) is a vibrant city in the central part of Zhejiang Province, China. While it might not boast the international fame of Hangzhou or Shanghai, Jinhua is a hidden gem waiting to be discovered, offering a delightful blend of historical charm, natural beauty, and a taste of authentic Zhejiang life.\nAs a city with a rich history stretching back over 2,200 years, Jinhua is a hub of culture and tradition. It is renowned for its ancient architecture, traditional cuisine, and serene landscapes. The city is surrounded by picturesque mountains, lush forests, and winding rivers, making it a paradise for nature lovers and hikers alike. At the same time, its bustling urban areas showcase the rapid economic growth and development typical of modern China.\nJinhua enjoys a humid subtropical climate, with warm, wet summers and mild winters. The best times to visit are spring (March to May) and autumn (September to November), when the weather is pleasant, and the scenery is at its most breathtaking.\nUnderstand.\nJinhua City is a prefecture-level city in Zhejiang Province, China. It serves as the central city and transportation hub of central Zhejiang. It is a National Historic and Cultural City and a well-known tourist destination. It serves as the central city and transportation hub of the region. Jinhua is a well-known tourist destination, rich in cultural heritage.\nAs one of Zhejiang's key cities, Jinhua has long been a center of politics, economy, and culture in the region. The city boasts a wealth of cultural heritage, with numerous ancient sites, historical buildings, and cultural landmarks. Surrounded by mountains, Jinhua offers picturesque natural landscapes that attract many tourists.\nToday, Jinhua is a vibrant and rapidly developing city. Despite the migration of many trade and industries to larger cities like Shanghai in recent decades, Jinhua has preserved its deep historical roots and traditional culture. The city's warm and humid climate makes spring and autumn the most ideal seasons for travel.\nAmong Jinhua's most famous attractions are the beautiful Dongyang woodcarving, the historic Double Dragon Cave, and the renowned Jinhua Ham, all of which provide visitors with a rich cultural experience. Additionally, the natural beauty of Jinhua, with its mountainous terrain and forest parks, offers plenty of outdoor activities, attracting adventure and hiking enthusiasts.\nCombining ancient culture and modern development, Jinhua is a memorable and unique travel destination in Zhejiang Province.\nHistory.\nJinhua, as a historic and cultural city in Zhejiang Province, boasts a long history and rich cultural heritage. The history of the Jinhua region can be traced back to over 3,000 years ago, during the late Neolithic period. Archaeologists have discovered many significant relics and artifacts in the area. Throughout ancient times, Jinhua was an important political, economic, and cultural center in Eastern Zhejiang, with a far-reaching influence in various dynasties.\nDuring the Han Dynasty (202 BCE - 220 CE), Jinhua was already an important administrative region, and it reached its peak during the Sui and Tang dynasties. In the Tang period, Jinhua prospered as a major transportation hub and commercial center. By the Song Dynasty, it became one of the political centers and a significant cultural city in Zhejiang.\nJinhua experienced the rise and fall of various dynasties. In particular, during the Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties, the city\u2019s economy developed gradually, forming a unique local economic structure. In the Qing period, Jinhua's silk and tea industries flourished, making it one of the most important economic and trade regions in China.\nIn modern history, Jinhua also occupies an important position. With the construction of railways and highways, it gradually developed into a transportation hub in central Zhejiang. Especially in the late 20th and early 21st centuries, with the rapid economic growth, Jinhua transformed into an industrial city focused on manufacturing, becoming a key economic growth point in Zhejiang Province.\nToday, Jinhua preserves many historical sites and cultural traditions, such as ancient temple fairs, traditional folk arts, and rich local festivals. These features make Jinhua a city where history and modernity coexist, attracting many visitors to explore its deep cultural heritage.\nClimate.\nJinhua, located in central Zhejiang Province, has a typical subtropical monsoon climate with distinct seasons and warm, humid weather year-round. During the spring and autumn, the weather is mild and pleasant, with daytime temperatures generally ranging from 20\u00b0C to 25\u00b0C and cooler nights, making it ideal for outdoor activities and travel. Summers are warm and humid, with temperatures reaching around 30\u00b0C, and rainfall is significant, especially from June to September, with frequent showers or thunderstorms. Due to Jinhua's hilly terrain, the summer heat is somewhat alleviated compared to lowland cities. Winter is warm and dry, with daytime temperatures ranging from 10\u00b0C to 20\u00b0C and cooler nights. Occasionally, cold fronts may bring a slight chill, but overall temperatures remain mild. With abundant rainfall throughout the year, the humidity is high, which supports lush plant growth, keeping the natural landscape green and vibrant all year round. Overall, Jinhua\u2019s climate is mild and pleasant, making it a great destination for travel at any time of the year.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nJinhua is an important transportation hub in Zhejiang Province, with several train stations. The Jinhua Railway Station and Jinhua South Railway Station are the two main stations. The Jinhua Railway Station, located in the city center, provides easy access to many cities. Jinhua South Railway Station, on the other hand, is a major hub for high-speed rail services, connecting to various cities across China. With the continuous expansion of the railway network, Jinhua\u2019s transportation infrastructure is improving, making it a convenient gateway to other cities in Zhejiang Province and the Yangtze River Delta region.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nJinhua is a well-connected city with several major bus stations, making it easy for visitors to explore the city and its surrounding areas. Here are some key bus stations in Jinhua:\nJinhua's bus system is extensive and affordable, making it the main mode of transport for both locals and visitors. The main bus stations, including the West, East, and South Bus Stations, connect Jinhua to various cities and destinations. The South Bus Station, in particular, serves as a major transport hub, offering services to several locations such as Yiwu, Yongkang, Wenzhou, and others.\ncity bus.\nJinhua's city bus system provides convenient travel options for both locals and visitors. There are several bus routes covering key areas of the city. Below are some of the main city bus routes:\nBus fares are affordable, typically ranging from 1 to 2 RMB, making it easy for visitors to move around Jinhua.\nBy bicycle.\nIn Jinhua, cycling is a convenient and environmentally friendly way to explore the city and its surrounding areas. Many major roads in the city are equipped with bike lanes, and traffic is generally not too heavy, making cycling a safe and pleasant option for getting around.\nSome hostels and hotels offer bicycle rentals for about \u00a520 per day, allowing visitors to rent traditional bikes. In addition, Jinhua has an increasing number of shared bicycles available for rent via mobile apps. The cost for using shared bikes typically starts at around \u00a51 per half hour. The shared bike system is very convenient, as users can pick up and drop off bikes at various locations around the city, especially in popular areas like city parks and tourist attractions.\nShared bikes require registration, typically needing a Chinese phone number or local ID for verification. This might limit accessibility for short-term foreign visitors unless they meet the requirements. However, once registered, it is a flexible way to explore the city at a leisurely pace, ideal for short-distance travel or sightseeing within the city.\nStay safe.\nJinhua is a relatively safe city, but like in any other place, visitors should stay alert, especially in busy commercial areas or crowded places, particularly at night. Avoid displaying valuables and be mindful of personal belongings."}
{"id": "208416", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208416", "title": "Bus travel in Mexico", "text": "Long-distance bus travel can be an interesting, inexpensive, and convenient way to see Mexico. This article describes only the long-distance inter-city bus services as well as inter-city buses that cross international borders. All major cities have their own urban bus transit systems that are described in the City articles. \nUnderstand.\nMexico has a world-class bus system, and service efficiency is on par with trains in Europe and East Asia. The route density of Mexican nationwide bus system surpasses the counterparts in the United States, Europe, and East Asia.\nBuses are without a doubt the backbone of personal intercity transport in Mexico because as of 2024 only 40% of Mexicans own a car, there is no high-speed rail in Mexico, trains mostly serve cargo and tourism purposes. Contarary to North America, buses also serve smaller villages.\nFor going from one city to another, the apps of CheckMyBus and Busbud show the departure times and the bus company.\nApps may be used to buy tickets on your cell phone. Larger bus companies have their own apps that can be used to buy tickets, check schedules, and more. It is often less hassle to buy tickets in person in the bus station, particularly if trying to make a bus connection from an arriving flight or traveling during peak travel periods like Christmas or the week before Easter. Tickets can be purchased in advance online or using the app, but tickets within 2 hours of departure must often need to be purchased in the bus station. Fares can also be paid in cash to the driver if you get on a bus along the way (rather than at the start of a route).\nClasses.\nBuses offer different levels of quality and comfort. Companies may use different terms for these classes, but there are generally three broad levels of service:\nOvernight buses.\nSome travelers avoid buses at night for security reasons or because they prefer to see the scenery as they travel. Some travelers though prefer night buses because they can sleep on the bus (saving the cost of a hotel room for a night), because they often cost less than buses departing during the day, or sometimes because they don't have much choice because they have a long journey or the only bus serving a route leaves at night. \nMany of the bus lines operate fewer (or sometimes no) buses at night, but others serve the night-traveling public with very comfortable buses that are often even more luxurious than their regular executive class buses. Although buses with true sleeper compartments are not common in Mexico, there are some excellent night buses. Omnibus de Mexico has some double-decker overnight buses with huge reclining seats that \"almost\" lie flat and that can provide a good night's sleep (especially on routes over 10 hours). The buses also feature individual screens for entertainment, jacks for headphones, and USB slots for keeping devices charged. ETN also has some very nice buses for overnight routes, like trips from Mexico City to Oaxaca or Puerto Escondido. Their big reclining seats have leg rests that pop up, making you feel almost like you're lying down flat.\nWhile daytime buses may still work best for you, there are some definite advantages to overnight buses as well.\nBus stations.\nBus stations in Mexico can be large or small, modern or dated. They are typically called \"Central de Autobuses\" or \"Terminal de Autobuses\". Small towns may have one or more company-owned terminals. These will typically have a small waiting room and are likely to have a taco vendor with a stand outside the terminal. In all bus terminals expect a small fee to be charged (typically M$5 to M$10) to use the rest rooms.\nLarge bus stations serve multiple bus companies, serve as transfer points more often than as final destinations, and may have dozens, or even hundreds of departures at any hour of the day. They often feel like a modern airport with large ticketing areas, waiting areas, and boarding areas. A large bus station typically offers ATMs or currency exchange service, food services and baggage check. Bus companies usually offer package delivery services in addition to passenger transport. Most bus stations have a counter where packages can be dropped off and picked up, though this service is sometimes substantial enough to be in its own building.\nConnections to local urban transportation varies. All bus terminals in Mexico City have an adjacent Metro station, and is useful, fast, and cheap for those passengers who travel light. Guadalajara and Monterrey also have Metro stations adjacent to the main bus station (Central de Camiones). Passengers with baggage will want to use taxis because Metro rules prohibit carrying large, bulky items. In Mexico City and other major bus terminals, taxis are regulated and authorized and only vetted taxis can pick up passengers. Arriving passengers who want a taxi will pay and provide destination information to someone at a central booth (or increasingly, at automated kiosks). You will be given a taxi ticket that you take outside to the taxi queue. (Uber and similar ride-hailing apps are often prohibited from picking up at the bus terminal, but you can get around that by walking down the street and using the app somewhere else).\nAirport bus terminals.\nMany of Mexico's busiest airports have their own bus terminals, making connections between cities easy and affordable. \nMexico City.\nMexico City is the de facto hub for bus transportation throughout the country. Journeys from virtually anywhere in Mexico can be made to anywhere else by building an itinerary with connections in Mexico City. The most important thing to know about using a long distance bus from Mexico City is that the city has four major bus stations (one for each point on the compass) and several less formal or company specific stops. All major stations have an adjacent Metro stop (though Metro rules prohibit carrying bulky baggage, so plan to use a taxi or Uber if you're traveling heavy). The major bus stations are:\nThe following table contains recommended terminals and bus lines to use for various inter-city destinations. The table includes only practical destinations within 12 hours of Mexico City (i.e., you could comfortably do it in one day). It does not include itineraries that would be stupid. For example, ADO has buses going to Cancun, but the journey takes over 24 hours and costs more than twice as much as a 90-minute flight on Viva Aerobus. Similarly, Cabo San Lucas would be a foolishly long bus trip involving either a trip around the Sea of Cortez or an overnight ferry ride between separate trips on different bus lines. \nMajor bus companies.\nThe companies listed below are some of the larger companies providing inter-city bus travel in Mexico. Some operate nationwide while others may focus on a specific region. No company holds a dominant market share, though some are dominant in a certain region. Several other small companies provide long-distance or regional bus travel. These are often listed in articles for a region, city or town.\nCross-border buses.\nYou can use buses to travel beyond Mexico to destinations in neighboring countries. \nBetween Mexico and United States.\nSome Mexican bus companies offer cross-border transportation between U.S. and Mexico as far north as Chicago and far south as Mexico City from the U.S./Mexico border areas. Some of the cross-border buses are subsidiaries of a larger Mexican bus company while others are smaller American- or Mexican-owned companies serving the regions near the border on one or both sides.\nConnections from Texas hubs to the Midwest including Chicago, the Southeast and Mexico are offered by Tornado Bus, El Expreso, 'and Omnibus Express. Service in and out of Florida is offered by the Chilean JetSet, Argentinian RedCoach, and Cuban-American La Cubana. In California and the Southwest operators include FuturaNet, Tufesa, [InterCalifornias] and El Paso-Los Angeles Limousines, which may have tickets starting from $1.\nBetween Mexico and Central America.\nOnward connections to/from Guatemala through Tapachula and to Belize through Chetumal. There are additional connections (by shuttle) between San Cristobal de las Casas and Antigua Guatemala (via Comitan, crossing through Ciudad Cuauht\u00e9moc/La Mesilla). The tickets on these shuttles are purchased from one of many agents in San Cristobal de las Casas. Passengers typically change vehicles at the border. The following offer regular first class pullman services out of Tapachula to Central America:\nSafety.\nFirst-time visitors to Mexico might wonder if it's safe to ride the buses in Mexico. These visitors sometimes come from countries where bus travel is a risky proposition, or they might come from a country with too many loud-mouth xenophobes spreading misinformation through their social media apps. Either way, a basic truth in Mexico is that long distance buses are among the safest means of getting around the country. \nAlthough incidents of buses being waylaid or passengers getting robbed have occurred, they occur on second-class buses that stop everywhere, let anyone on, and travel on secondary highways and back roads. Incidents do not usually happen on first-class or executive-class buses (which are the ones described in this article, and are the best choice for international travelers.) \nMillions of travelers criss-cross Mexico by bus every year. Doing so is far safer than driving a private car, cheaper, and usually more comfortable and convenient. "}
{"id": "208418", "revid": "76122", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208418", "title": "Sakurai", "text": "Sakurai (\u685c\u4e95) is a city in Nara, Japan.\nUnderstand.\nSakurai is an ancient city that was briefly capital of Japan in 457\u2013479. It has numerous imperial burial mounds (\"kofun\") and several ancient temples and shrines.\nGet in.\n is the terminus of the JR Sakurai Line from Nara, about 40 minutes away. It can also be reached from Osaka's Namba station on the Kintetsu Osaka Line in about 1 hour.\nAlternatively, the ancient Yamanobe-no-michi (\u5c71\u8fba\u306e\u9053) trail from Tenri, halfway to Nara, traverses 11 km through the countryside to Sakurai. Both trailheads offer bicycle rental and are easily reached on the JR Sakurai line."}
{"id": "208425", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208425", "title": "Tongchuan", "text": "Tongchuan is a city in the central part of Shaanxi Province, at the junction of the Guanzhong Plain and the Northern Shaanxi Plateau. It lies to the north of Xi'an and is a key stop on the way to the birthplace of Chinese civilization, the Mausoleum of the Yellow Emperor, and the revolutionary holy land of Yan'an. \nThe rich historical heritage has endowed Tongchuan with unique tourism resources. The blending of natural beauty and cultural heritage reflects the city's distinctive tourism characteristics.\nUnderstand.\nTongchuan combines cultural depth with natural beauty. As a key stop on the way to Yan'an and the Mausoleum of the Yellow Emperor, Tongchuan holds significant geographical and historical importance.\nThe city's terrain transitions from the low hills and mountains in the north to the Guanzhong Plain in the south. It has a temperate continental climate with pleasant springs and autumns, hot summers, and cold winters. Tongchuan is famous for its scenic spots such as Yuhua Palace and Yaowang Mountain, making it not only a city rich in cultural heritage but also a great destination for leisure and tourism.\nThe name \"Tongchuan\" originates from \"Tongshui zhi chuan\" (the river of copper water). Initially named \"Tongguan,\" it was later renamed Tongchuan due to its phonetic similarity to \"Tongguan.\" The city's history dates back to the Qin and Han dynasties, and it reached its peak during the Tang Dynasty, becoming an important cultural hub where multiple cultures converged.\nHistory.\nTongchuan is famous for the Yuhua Palace, one of the four major palaces during the Tang Dynasty. Additionally, the Yaozhou Kiln is one of the most significant ceramic kilns in Chinese history, contributing greatly to the development of Chinese ceramic art. The Zhaojin Revolutionary Base in Tongchuan was established by revolutionary figures such as Liu Zhidan and Xie Zichang and served as a key revolutionary base in northwest China, making outstanding contributions to the modern Chinese revolution.\nTongchuan is rich in mineral resources, particularly known for its coal and ceramic clay. The city is surrounded by mountains, with the northern region showcasing natural ecological landscapes, while the southern part is characterized by historical and cultural significance. Tongchuan has a long-standing cultural heritage. Yaowang Mountain, not only the place where the famous Tang Dynasty physician Sun Simiao practiced, is also one of the birthplaces of Taoist culture. The ceramic craftsmanship of Yaozhou Kiln has spanned over a thousand years and is still in use today. Tongchuan\u2019s folk artists are renowned for traditional handicrafts such as paper-cutting and embroidery, which are commonly found in the local souvenir markets.\nClimate.\nTongchuan has a temperate continental climate, with abundant sunshine. Winters are cold, and summers are hot. The annual average temperature is approximately 10\u00b0C to 12\u00b0C. Spring is warm and dry, with flowers blooming everywhere, making it an ideal time for hiking and flower viewing. Summer is hot, but the mornings and evenings are cool, and the mountainous areas offer a perfect escape for the summer heat. Autumn features cool weather, clear skies, and is the peak tourist season, with the mountains adorned in vibrant autumn colors, creating breathtaking scenery. Winter is cold and dry, but the snow-covered scenic spots, such as Yuhua Palace and Yaowang Mountain, offer a unique charm, especially suited for ice and snow activities.\nTongchuan's annual rainfall is approximately 600 millimeters, with the majority of precipitation occurring from July to September during the summer months. Travelers should be prepared for rain during this period. Due to the variations in terrain, the climate conditions differ across different areas of Tongchuan. The new district and old city areas are relatively warm, while Yijun County and the surrounding mountainous areas are cooler, making them ideal for summer trips to escape the heat.\nGet in.\nIt is 68 kilometers from Xi'an city center and 72 kilometers from Xi'an Xianyang Airport. \nBy plane.\nXi'an Xianyang International Airport is the nearest major airport to Tongchuan. It connects to many domestic and international cities. The airport is about 72 kilometers from downtown Tongchuan. Taxis and buses are available for easy transfers to Tongchuan \u2014 about 1.5 hours to reach Tongchuan city.\nThe airport is well-connected with major cities in China, including Beijing, Shanghai, and Chengdu, and international routes to destinations in Asia. To reach Tongchuan, you can take a direct airport shuttle bus or a taxi. A taxi ride takes approximately 1.5 hours, and the cost is around \u00a5150-200. Shuttle buses also operate between the airport and Tongchuan, providing an affordable alternative for travelers.\nBy train.\nXi'an North Railway Station is the nearest major railway station to Tongchuan, located about 68 kilometers away. Xi'an North Station is a high-speed rail hub connecting many major cities within China. High-speed trains from this station connect to destinations such as Beijing, Shanghai, Chengdu, and Xi'an, and there are several routes to other provinces.\nFrom Xi'an North Railway Station, travelers can easily take a bus or taxi to Tongchuan. The bus ride takes around 1 hour, and taxis are available for a more direct and comfortable option, with fares typically ranging from \u00a550-\u00a5100.\nBy bus.\nCopper is well-connected to neighboring cities and regions via several highways, including the Xitong Expressway (G65W), which is the main route connecting the city to the rest of Shaanxi province.\nCopper's main bus station serves various long-distance routes, connecting the city with both provincial and neighboring areas. The city has several bus stations offering services:\nThe road network in and around Tongchuan is well-developed, with frequent bus services to and from nearby cities. Here are some of the popular long-distance routes:\nThere are also local shuttle buses connecting Tongchuan to nearby towns and villages within the city and surrounding areas. These buses are very affordable, with fares generally ranging from \u00a510-\u00a530 depending on the distance.\nBy car.\nDriving is one of the best ways to explore Tongchuan and its surrounding areas, especially for travelers who enjoy flexible travel schedules. Here are a few recommended routes:\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nTongchuan has an extensive public transportation network, providing convenient access to key areas of the city and surrounding scenic spots. The bus services are affordable, with ticket prices generally ranging from \u00a51 to \u00a52. Here are some of the main bus routes and specialized services available in the city:\nBy taxi.\nTaxis in Tongchuan start at \u00a55. The fare is generally affordable, and for most trips within the city center, the cost typically does not exceed \u00a520. However, it's important to ensure the driver uses the meter to avoid any potential overcharging, especially on short trips. Larger taxis may cost slightly more than smaller ones, but in general, the fare system is standardized and reasonable.Be aware that taxis in Tongchuan are zoned, and drivers may refuse to go to certain areas outside their designated zone. If you are traveling to a more remote or less accessible area, you may encounter this limitation. Additionally, there might be a peak hour when taxis are less available, particularly around 6:00 PM when many drivers change shifts.\nIn Tongchuan, you can easily book a ride through popular ride-hailing platforms such as Didi Chuxing (\u6ef4\u6ef4\u51fa\u884c), which is widely used across the city. This is especially useful for travelers needing transportation to remote areas, tourist spots, or when taxis are in high demand.\nBy bicycle and e-bike.\nBicycles and electric bikes (e-bikes) are available for rent in several areas of Tongchuan, with rental prices typically starting around \u00a510-\u00a520 per day for bicycles. The city is relatively flat, and there are bike lanes along many major roads, making cycling a pleasant and practical option for getting around, especially for short trips or sightseeing.\nIn addition to traditional bike rentals, Tongchuan has a growing number of shared bicycles and e-bikes, which can be rented via popular mobile apps. These bikes are conveniently available at various locations around the city, including scenic areas and transportation hubs. The cost for renting a shared bike or e-bike typically starts at around \u00a51 per half hour, with e-bikes slightly more expensive due to their electric charge. The bikes can be easily picked up and dropped off using the app, which also offers real-time availability.To use the shared bike system, you must register via the app, which requires a Chinese phone number or local ID for verification. While this system is convenient and affordable, it may be less accessible to foreign tourists without local verification. However, for those with access to the service, it provides a flexible and eco-friendly way to explore the city.\nRecommended cycling routes:\nBy car.\nCopper\u2019s road conditions are generally good, making self-driving one of the recommended ways to explore the city and surrounding areas, especially for those wishing to visit suburban and mountainous attractions.\nParking:\nCar rental services:\nRecommended driving routes:\nGo next.\nCross-regional routes recommended"}
{"id": "208427", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208427", "title": "Sanya Phoenix International Airport", "text": "Sanya Phoenix International Airport (referred to as \"Phoenix Airport\") (\u4e09\u4e9a\u51e4\u51f0\u56fd\u9645\u673a\u573a)is located in Fenghuang Village, Yanglang Town, in the northwest of Sanya City, Hainan Province, about 14 kilometers east of Sanya City Center and 5 kilometers west of Tianya Haijiao Tourist Scenic Area. It is the hub airport of China Eastern Airlines, China Southern Airlines, Hainan Airlines, and is also one of the hub airports of Oneworld.\nUnderstand.\nPhoenix Airport was rated as a four-star airport by Skytrax, and was selected as \"World's Best Regional Airport in Asia Pacific Second Place\", \"World's Best Airport (Same Region and Size)\" and other honors in April 2016. In March 2017, it won the second place in ASQ passenger satisfaction for airports with 15-25 million passengers in 2016, which fully shows that Phoenix Airport has been widely recognized by all sectors of society.\nIt has been awarded a series of honorary titles such as \"Top Ten Economic Leaders of the First Hainan International Tourism Island\", \"Advanced Unit for Supporting the Annual Meeting of the Boao Forum for Asia\", \"National Aviation Service with the Most Public Satisfaction\", \"National User Satisfaction Enterprise\", ACI \"Asia Pacific Best Improvement Award Airport\", Skytrax \"World's Best VIP Terminal\" by relevant departments.\nFlights.\nPhoenix Airport began operations on July 1, 1994. The airport\u2019s route network covers major provincial capitals and key cities in China, as well as important countries and regions along the \"Silk Road Economic Belt\" and the \"21st Century Maritime Silk Road,\" including Hong Kong, Russia, South Korea, Central Asia, East Asia, and Southeast Asia. This has led to a relatively well-established flight network layout.\nGround transportation.\nPhoenix Airport offers public shuttle buses that operate between the T1 terminal, T2 terminal, International Terminal, and the VIP terminal.\nBy bus.\nSanya has over sixty bus routes, operating from around 06:00 to 23:00. The ticket prices follow a distance-based fare system, ranging from \u00a52 to 20. In addition, there are many privately-operated shuttle buses, and many hotels (resorts) offer free sightseeing buses to the city center and tourist attractions, making travel very convenient.\nPhoenix Airport is one of the key transportation hubs in Sanya\u2019s network. Bus routes passing through Phoenix Airport include routes 27, 29, 33, 36, 43, and 8. Additionally, there are Phoenix Airport Bus Line 1 and Phoenix Airport Bus Line 3.\nIf you prefer not to take the airport shuttle bus, you can use any of the public bus routes, with most ticket prices around \u00a53-4. Bus routes 8 and 27 take approximately 50 minutes to reach Shui Li Building.\nBy taxi.\nPrice.\nThe taxis in Sanya are dark blue and orange. The starting price is 8 yuan, and 2 yuan is charged for each kilometer after the starting distance of 2 kilometers.\nNotes.\nIf you have any opinions or suggestions on taxi operations, please call the Sanya Transportation Administration Complaint Hotline: 0898-88667803.\nBy high-speed train (Hainan West Ring Railway).\nIt is not recommended to take the high-speed train to Sanya Station due to the heavy flow of passengers at the station.\nNear the airport, there is the Hainan West Ring Railway (high-speed rail), which can take you to Sanya Station.\nBy tram.\nThe T1 tram line starts at Phoenix Airport and ends at Jiangang Road. It passes through Airport Road, Haihong Road, Sanya Bay Road, Jinjiling Road, and Shengli Road.\nWait.\nPhoenix Airport has a paid VIP lounge, which is open to all passengers. Passengers can take a break in the public lounge while waiting to board or transfer, enjoy delicious food and drinks, browse the Internet and check emails, and read a variety of newspapers and magazines.\nEat and drink.\nThere are various restaurants in Phoenix Airport, inside the airport and in nearby villages. \nBuy.\nIf you need to kill time, Phoenix Airport has a wide variety of shops.\nIn the international departure hall, there is a small branch of a large duty-free shop: China Duty Free Shop (Phoenix International Airport Branch). After passing security, go to the departure hall and the shop will be on your left.\nThere is no duty-free shop for domestic departures, only the pick-up area of the world's largest single duty-free shop - Sanya Haitang Bay Duty Free Shopping Center, which is opposite the China Southern Airlines VIP Room in the quarantine area.\nYou can pick up the goods at the airport after buying them here, but you must have a departing ticket to enjoy the discount, otherwise you will have to pay a 20% tax. For more details, please visit Duty Free Shop Official Website[dead link] for inquiries. The Duty Free Shop Customer Service Hotline is: 400-699-6956.\nNearby.\nTransfer.\nSince Sanya Phoenix International Airport is small in size, the number of flights is very busy. Whether it is domestic flights or Hong Kong, Macau, Taiwan/international flights, many people will not be able to book tickets during the holidays. It is recommended that you buy tickets one month in advance.\nIf you cannot book a ticket, you can try to use Haikou Meilan International Airport as a transfer.\nThen transfer to the Hainan Ring Island High-speed Railway or Hainan East Ring High-speed Railway to Sanya at Meilan Station and go to Sanya Station. Then transfer to other means of transportation to the city.\nThere is no need to take the high-speed rail to the Phoenix Airport Station of Hainan West Ring Expressway, because Phoenix Airport Station is about 14 kilometers away from the center of Sanya, and there may be traffic jams for about an hour on the way to the city center.\nIn addition to Haikou Meilan International Airport, other Hainan airports can also be used, because other Hainan cities are relatively backward, and the air tickets will be cheaper.\nOperations.\nSecurity.\nThe \"Traffic Police Brigade of the Public Security Bureau of Phoenix Airport in Sanya\" operates under the leadership of the Sanya Public Security Bureau. It also receives guidance, inspections, and supervision from national aviation security authorities and their local offices. The brigade is responsible for aviation security, public safety, and traffic management within the Phoenix Airport jurisdiction. It is permanently stationed at Sanya Phoenix International Airport, the Security Service Desk (T2 Terminal, F2), the Phoenix Airport Sub-Bureau of the Sanya Public Security Bureau, and the Airport Police Station of the same bureau.\nVisa.\nBusinesses in Sanya benefit from the tax incentives offered by the Hainan Special Economic Zone. Passengers and cargo transiting through Hainan are eligible for certain transportation fare discounts. Citizens of countries with diplomatic relations with China can apply for \"visa on arrival\" at Hainan ports with an ordinary passport. Additionally, group tourists from certain countries are granted visa-free entry for up to 15 days."}
{"id": "208433", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208433", "title": "East Central Regional Rail Trail", "text": "East Central Regional Rail Trail is a recreational trail (and former rail trail) in Volusia County and the Space Coast that constitutes a part of Florida's Coast-to-Coast Trail. A paved trail, the route is popular with cyclists, as it crosses a variety of ecosystems and scenic areas including cypress groves, forests, and pine flatwoods. The trail is approximately 50 mi from its westernmost point in Enterprise (near Deltona) to its easternmost point in Titusville, and is open to hikers and cyclists.\nUnderstand.\nThe trail is part of the Coast-to-Coast Trail, a project started in the 2000s, to construct a paved, multiuse recreational trail from Florida's Gulf Coast to the Atlantic Ocean. For funding and logistical purposes, the trail was split into several smaller trails with cooperation with local county and regional authorities. The East Central Regional Rail Trail is among the longest of these trails and is one of the earliest completed segments, with 50 mi of trail constructed and dedicated in the 2010s.\nAs its name implies, the rail trail follows the route of one of the many railroads that historically crisscrossed Florida during its agricultural boom of the late 19th and 20th centuries. Much of the route parallels the Osteen-Maytown Road and therefore includes multiple parking lots/staging areas to access different parts of the trail. Notably, the trail fork into two tributaries about halfway along its route, with the southern fork going to Titusville (providing a link to the Intracoastal Waterway and Canaveral National Seashore) and the northern fork going to Edgewater.\nSome parts of the trail have intermittent benches and road markings for cyclists, while other parts are merely pavement, as the route was constructed in short segments across several years. The Titusville fork of the trail, for example, includes semi-regular roundabouts enabling cyclists to make u-turns without stopping, while the Osteen-Maytown segment of the trail is elevated with several bridges over creeks. Being a rail trail, there are dirt banks along the side of the path for much of its length, and the trail is raised above its surroundings, which are swamp for much of the route.\nPrepare.\nVisit during the winter because heat in summer makes cycling or hiking on this route difficult. Much of the path isn't under shade, and the pavement gets heated by direct sunlight. Consider distances; some parts of the path are longer than they appear on a map, but there are signs along the way that indicate half-mile markers, distances to different segments and parking, and region maps.\nGet in.\nAll access points to the trail are parking lots accessible by car. The nearest passenger train station is the. US-17 connects the station to the, which accesses and the East Central Regional Rail Trail.\nFor more information on the Coast-to-Coast Trail, which includes the Spring-to-Spring Trail and the East Central Regional Rail Trail, see the Florida Department of Environmental Protection website. However, this map lacks information regarding staging points and access areas to the trail and shows only the route, with completed parts of the route in green and incomplete parts of the route in dotted purple. Volusia County provides a map of access points as of the path's planning stage in 2009.\nGo.\nFollowing the trail from west to east:\nTitusville fork.\nThe easternmost point of the trail is in central Titusville. There is parking in downtown Titusville but no parking at the trailhead.\nThe best options for food and lodging along the route can be found in Deltona, Sanford, and Titusville.\nEdgewater fork.\nAfter exiting the Maytown Spur, the Edgewater fork of the trail follows an underpass under I-95 and turns north toward Edgewater, crossing the Osteen-Maytown Rd with a marked crosswalk (though beware of fast cars on the road).\nStay safe.\nAlligators and bears are common wildlife along the route. Alligators typically inhabit waterways and won't be dangerously near as long as you stick to the path. Black bears are more of a concern; see dangerous animals for further information.\nSome parts of the trail aren't close to public view or emergency services. In particular, the segment of the trail on the Maytown Spur between Maytown and Titusville isn't adjacent to any public roads or access routes.\nThe trail includes regular markers (mile or half-mile) showing the distance to the next segment of the path, the coordinates, the county, and the name of the regional trail, although the exact appearance and details of these markers vary depending upon the segment of the route. Markers also include emergency (911) and non-emergency numbers to call in case of need for assistance."}
{"id": "208435", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208435", "title": "Burra (South Australia)", "text": "Burra is a town of 920 (2021) in South Australia's Clare Valley region. It is most known for its Cornish mines, which have been on the tentative list for UNESCO World Heritage since 2024.\nGet in.\nBurra is 2 hours north of Adelaide (about 166 km from Adelaide CBD) via the M2 Northern Expressway. Continue on until you reach A20 Gawler Ring Road and then exit onto A32 Barrier Hwy/B82 Horrocks Hwy. Stay on A32 Barrier Hwy for 110 km (about 80 minutes) until you reach Burra. From Port Wakefield, it's an 80-minute drive east via B84 and A32."}
{"id": "208436", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208436", "title": "Takatsuki", "text": "Takatsuki (\u9ad8\u69fb) is a city in northeastern Osaka prefecture, near Kyoto. The neighboring rural districts of Shimamoto (\u5cf6\u672c) and Oyamazaki (\u5927\u5c71\u5d0e), just across the border in Kyoto, are also covered here.\nUnderstand.\nLocated exactly halfway between Osaka and Kyoto, Takatsuki is a popular commuter town for people working in both cities, but most visitors to Japan only see it in passing from a train window. However, if you can't decide whether to stay in Osaka or Kyoto and want to get a bit off the beaten tourist trail, you could definitely do worse than stay here.\nGet in.\nTakatsuki is bisected by both the JR Tokaido-Sanyo Line (JR Osaka to JR Kyoto stations) and the Hankyu Kyoto Line (Umeda to Kyoto-Kawaramachi). Their respective stations, JR's (\u9ad8\u69fb\u99c5) and Hankyu's (\u9ad8\u69fb\u5e02\u99c5), are a few city blocks apart. Travel time to either city on either line is 15-20 minutes.\nSleep.\nIn addition to the usual business hotels near Takatsuki station, there are a scattering of hot spring resorts."}
{"id": "208455", "revid": "2363356", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208455", "title": "Ali (Tibet)", "text": "Ali (\u963f\u91cc) is the capital of Ngari prefecture in far western Tibet. Confusingly, the town is also known by its Tibetan name Ngari, its county name Gar (\u5676\u5c14 \"Ga'er\"), or after the neighboring river, which is Shiquanhe (\u72ee\u6cc9\u6cb3) in Chinese and S\u00eangg\u00eazangbo in Tibetan.\nUnderstand.\nAli is a Chinese-built town of around 25,000 people on the banks of the Sengge Zangbo River. As it was created only in 1965 to serve as the prefectural capital, there are few sights of interest in town, which serves mostly as a staging post for visitors to Mount Kailash, \"only\" about 330 km away by road.\nThe city lies at an altitude of 4,255 meters, 600 meters higher up than Lhasa. It has a cold desert climate with minimal rainfall, and sub-zero temperatures are possible throughout the year.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\n (\u963f\u91cc\u6606\u838e\u673a\u573a,), 70 km south of Ali, has regular flights to Lhasa and Kashgar.\nBy car.\nNational Highway G219 runs from Kashgar in Xinjiang (1300 km) through Ali to Shigatse and Lhasa (1460 km). The Xinjiang to Tibet portion of the route is extremely remote, notoriously rough and requires a slew of additional permits.\nProvincial route S301 also connects Ali to Nagqu, but this route is rarely if ever taken by travellers."}
{"id": "208463", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208463", "title": "Liverdun", "text": "Liverdun is a commune in Lorraine containing medieval hill village of the same name, that overlooks the Moselle River as it cuts way through its tree lined limestone gorge.\nUnderstand.\nLiverdun is known for its \u201cMadeleines de Liverdun\u201d, from the Chenel biscuit factory which was taken over in February 2015 by Vincent Ferry from the Meuse company \"Clair de Lorraine\" located in Void Vacon which sells local products.\nIt 2022, it was home to 5,700 people.\nDo.\nThere are several walks starting from the village along the Moselle and through the woods.\nBuy.\nBy the station there is a shop which sells Madeleines, the small cake which made the town famous."}
{"id": "208464", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208464", "title": "Ahuacatl\u00e1n", "text": "Ahuacatl\u00e1n is a small town in Nayarit lying at the foot of the Ceboruco volcano. It is one of Mexico's designated Pueblos M\u00e1gicos.\nUnderstand.\nAhuacatl\u00e1n was historically an important stop along the \"Camino Real\" (Royal Road) between Guadalajara and the port at San Blas. The town has a population of about 7,500 people (2020).\nGet in.\nThe nearest large commercial airport is Guadalajara (). \nBy bus.\nOmnibus de Mexico operates several daily buses from Guadalajara to Ahuactlan, leaving the main bus terminal about every 2 hours. The 1 hour and 45-minute trip will cost about M$400. \nBy car.\nFrom Guadalajara, drive northwest on federal highway MEX-15D. This is a toll road in good condition. The 140 km trip will take about 2 hours.\nEat.\nThe town is known for producing \"mango cajeta\", a traditional sweet candy."}
{"id": "208477", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208477", "title": "Rajaji National Park", "text": "Rajaji National Park is in the Garhwal region of Uttarakhand in the Himalayan North region of India. It has been named as the second tiger reserve in Uttarakhand after the Corbett Tiger Reserve.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThree sanctuaries \u2014 Rajaji, Motichur, and Chilla \u2014 were merged to form this national park.\nFlora.\nRajaji National Park is nestled between the Shivalik ranges and the Indo-Gangetic plains. Broadleaved deciduous forests, riverine and riparian vegetation, scrubland, grasslands and pine forests form the range of botanical habitats in the park. \nFauna.\nRajaji National Park is at the northwestern limit of distribution for both the Indian elephant and Bengal tiger in India. The goral and the Himalayan tahr are also noteworthy residents, mainly staying on the precipitous, pine-covered slopes. Big herds of chital also inhabit the park, sometimes numbering as many as 250 individuals in a herd. Sambar, swamp deer, Northern red muntjac, Indochinese hog deer, nilgai, wild boar and sloth bear also occur in the park, and rhesus macaque and the common langur are fairly common. The Bengal tiger and Indian leopard are the prime predators in Rajaji National Park, apart from leopard cat, jungle cat, dhole, several viverrine species, Bengal fox, golden jackal and yellow-throated marten. The Himalayan black bear inhabits the higher reaches of the park. \nOver 315 species of birds are found in the park, whereas the wider region has over 500 species of birds, including both residents and migrants.\nThe rivers which flow through the park harbour species of fish such as trout and mahseer.\nDo.\nIn any national park, most people opt for jungle safaris. This includes renting a jeep, driven by a driver, where you can sit and spot wild animals.\nFor bird enthusiasts, this destination offers a chance to glimpse a range of rare and exotic bird species, including Greater Scaup, White-naped Woodpecker, Scaly Thrush, Snowy-browned Flycatcher, Great Hornbill, and Black-bellied Tern.\nStay safe.\nThis area remains sensitive due to the presence of wild animals. Please strictly follow the guidelines provided by the authorities. Avoid wearing vibrant or bright clothing and do not carry food or drink items with you. Return to your place before sunset."}
{"id": "208480", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208480", "title": "Tetela de Ocampo", "text": "Tetela de Ocampo is a small town in the eastern Mexico state of Puebla. It is rich in history with its early mining and wrought iron industries during the colonial era, involvement in the Mexican War of Independence, and key roles fighting the French army in the French incursion of 1862. The town is also a magnet for outdoors activities including hiking, climbing, and exploring nearby waterfalls, canyons, and cave systems.\nUnderstand.\nThe town was originally called \"Tetela de Oro\" due to its gold mining history, it is generally known simply as \"Tetela\". It has a population of about 5,600 (2020). The town has been designated as one of Mexico's Pueblos M\u00e1gicos.\nBattle of Puebla.\nTetela de Ocampo was the home base for the Sixth Battalion of National Guards and most of its soldiers and officers were born and raised in Tetela. These soldiers were among the first to face the French army at Loreto and Guadalupe hills. In 2020, the state Congress declared the town to be \"Tres Veces Heroica Tetela de Ocampo\" (thrice heroic).\nOn April 8, 2020, the Magical Town was declared the \u201cTres Veces Heroica Tetela de Ocampo\u201d (Thrice Heroic Tetela de Ocampo) by the state congress.\nGet in.\nThe nearest airport with commercial service is Puebla International Airport (), which receives flights from the United States on United Airlines and from major Mexican cities and resorts on Aeromexico, Viva Aerobus, and Volaris.\nBuses to Tetela operate frequently from both the Puebla CAPU bus station and from the Mexico City TAPO bus station. Buses are operated by ATAH and Verdes.\nGet around.\nThe historic downtown (Centro) is most easily navigated by walking, though riding a bike can be a fun way to get the lay of the land. If you need to get further out, particularly to explore some of the nearby natural attractions, your hotel can call a taxi or you can find taxis waiting at a sitio next to the Zocalo."}
{"id": "208481", "revid": "2442520", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208481", "title": "ATM fees", "text": "ATM providers can charge withdrawal fees for their services. They can be either a percentage of the amount withdrawn, a fixed fee, or a combination of both. Fees can also vary for Mastercard and Visa cards. This page focuses on ATM fees charged to foreign cardholders by ATM operators. This includes prepaid cards such as Revolut or Wise. Please note that your bank or card issuer can also charge their fees independently of ATM providers. \nA mobile app such as ATM Fee Saver can also be used to get an estimated amount of ATM fees for a given withdrawal amount. \n\"See Money:Card costs for a more detailed explanation of the fees you could be charged.\""}
{"id": "208494", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=208494", "title": "Kew (New South Wales)", "text": "Kew is a town of 1,800 (2021) along the Pacific Hwy in the Mid-North Coast region of New South Wales.\nGet in.\nKew is on the A1 Pacific Hwy with exits on both sides of the highway. Kew is about 30 minutes (~50 km) north of Taree and 15 minutes south of Thrumster (Port Macquarie on/offramp).\nThere is a train station in neighbouring Kendall, but it is a 30-minute walk and there's very little reason to visit Kew without a car.\nGet around.\nMost of Kew's points of interests are on the single main street through town, Nancy Bird Walton Dr. The town is fairly walkable in the sense that everything is just a few minutes of each other, though it isn't necessarily pedestrian-friendly."}
