{"id": "210039", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=210039", "title": "Tortosa", "text": "Tortosa is in Tarragona (province), and the Ebro Way of St. James crosses the city.\nUnderstand.\nTortosa, situated on the Ebro River, is a city rich in history and cultural heritage. With over two thousand years of history, it has been influenced by various civilizations, including Romans, Moors, and Christians. The city features architectural treasures like the Suda Castle and the Cathedral of Santa Maria. Visitors can explore the Jewish Quarter, enjoy the Renaissance Festival, and experience local cuisine. Surrounded by natural landscapes like the Els Ports Natural Park, and the UNESCO Biosphere Reserve \" Terres de l\u2019Ebre, Catalonia\", Tortosa offers a blend of historical sites, cultural events, and outdoor activities. \nSee.\nTortosa offers a rich historical experience with several must-see sites:\nConnect.\nAs of April 2026, Tortosa and its approach roads have 5G from all Spanish carriers."}
{"id": "210044", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=210044", "title": "Banggai Islands", "text": "The Banggai Islands are an archipelago in the Banda Sea, off the east coast of Central Sulawesi, Indonesia.\nRegions.\nThe Banggai Islands consist of two separate regencies: the Banggai Islands Regency, which includes the largest island of Peleng, and the Banggai Laut Regency, which contains Banggai Island and some smaller, more remote islands off to the southwest and southeast.\nUnderstand.\nStill very underdeveloped touristically, the Banggai Islands are a place to escape the tourist masses from Tana Toraja and the Togian Islands. This makes the Banggai Islands a little harder to travel in without a guide or Indonesian language skills. Most visitors will stick to the handful of scattered homestays along the typical routes and receive lots of smiles and planning assistance from the owners, who all seem to know each other.\nGet in.\nBy boat.\nThere are daily boats from Luwuk to Leme-Leme on west Peleng island at 2PM, and daily boats in the other direction at 9AM. Similar daily boats serve Luwuk - Salakan (east Peleng island) and Luwuk - Banggai (Banggai Laut).\nTwice a week (presumably Tuesday and Friday) there is also a direct boat at 7AM from Luk Panenteng (where Paisu Pok Lake is) to Luwuk and back. The tickets can't be bought online, and depending on your chosen boat company, can't even be bought a day before departure. Just show up a few hours before departure at the pier.\nBy plane.\nAlthough there is a new airport on Banggai Laut, as of March 2025, there did not seem to be any flights there. The closest airport thus still is Luwuk (see \"By boat\" above for how to arrive from there).\nDo.\nSnorkelling and enjoying the beautiful empty beaches, lagoons and lakes.\nFor scuba diving, there may be some dive centers in Luwuk offering trips to the Banggai Islands.\nEat.\nThere are not many public restaurants on the Banggai Islands: even in Salakan the options are somewhat limited.\nThe accommodations will usually provide three meals per day."}
{"id": "210048", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=210048", "title": "T\u00e0rrega", "text": "T\u00e0rrega is in Lleida (province), and the Catalan Way of St. James crosses the city. \nUnderstand.\nThe origins of T\u00e0rrega date back to the mid-11th century, when the Count of Barcelona Ramon Berenguer I conquered its castle. During the 19th century it saw the inauguration of the Manresa-T\u00e0rrega-Lleida railway line (1862), the granting of the title of city by Alfonso XII (1884), the beginnings of construction of a series of basic infrastructures such as electricity and telephone, and the development of regional roads."}
{"id": "210051", "revid": "771685", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=210051", "title": "Solsona", "text": "Solsona is in Lleida province. The city is known for its Gothic cathedral, Romanesque bell tower, and the Episcopal Palace. Solsona is also famous for its giant festivals, earning it the title of \"city of giants.\" It offers a tranquil atmosphere and warm hospitality, making it an attractive destination for those interested in history and cultural traditions.\nUnderstand.\nSolsona boasts a rich historical and cultural heritage. Founded in the 10th century around a castle and monastery, it retains its medieval charm with cobblestone streets and historic buildings. \nDo.\nMTB routes.\ndetailed information at tourist office"}
